## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why This Breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it no longer works. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`).

---

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on cancel | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout)` |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue to loop condition check
                // Loop will exit because token.IsCancellationRequested is true
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 5 seconds for the thread to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

        // If thread is still alive after timeout, it's stuck in uncooperative code.
        // There is NO equivalent to Thread.Abort in modern .NET.
        // Log a warning — the IsBackground=true flag ensures process exit kills it.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes to Supporting Methods

```csharp
// BEFORE: may block indefinitely with no way to interrupt
private Item GetNextItem() { /* blocking call */ }

// AFTER: accepts token so it can be interrupted
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // If using a BlockingCollection<T>:
    return _queue.Take(token);

    // If using a custom wait:
    // token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout) instead of Thread.Sleep
    // token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested() at checkpoints
}
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| **Not passing token to `GetNextItem`** | If the method blocks (e.g., `queue.Take()`), cancellation never reaches it — thread hangs forever |
| **`Thread.Sleep` in loops** | Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` — Sleep ignores cancellation |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers and registered callbacks |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` filter** | Could accidentally swallow unrelated cancellation from inner calls |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It's cooperative — only takes effect at the next checkpoint. Design accordingly |
| **Relying on `Thread.Abort` as a safety net for hung threads** | No replacement exists. Move truly uncooperative work to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` |

---

### If `GetNextItem` Cannot Be Modified

If the blocking call is in third-party code that doesn't accept a `CancellationToken`, your only option for hard termination is to run it in a **separate process** and use `Process.Kill()`. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET.
